On Mar 3, 9:40 pm, "nmi" <iverson.n...@gmail.com> wrote:
> It seems everyone on RGRN loves alchemy of potions. It certainly makes
> the game easier, doesn't it. So why not implement a conduct? Here are
> some ideas:
>
> 1) No dipping of potions into things
> 2) No swimming to dilute potions
> 3) No dipping of objects into potions.
> 4) No polymorph of potions.
>
> I would be in favor of 1+2+3+4 = did not perform alchemy
> or the silly1+3=avoided dipping.
>
> What do you think? Is this yet another stupid ascension trick?

To me, at least, "did not perform alchemy" = refraining from dipping
potions into other non-water potions.

The word "alchemy" would seem to imply the involvement of some kind of
magical element; by contrast, using water to dilute things is rather
mundane, and using (un)holy water to mess with something's bcu status
seems more of a religious practice.  In any case, I can do either of
those first two to my glass of orange juice right now if I want to;
"alchemy" sounds (to me) like much more of a mystical -- and almost
definitionally magic-dependent -- practice that's only possible in
fantasy worlds such as that in which Nethack is set.

On the other hand, dipping weird combinations of potions into each
other to get new potions is certainly magical, and I think an alchemy
conduct could be interesting if defined as I stated (i.e. didn't dip
potions into any non-water potions).

This is an interesting idea in any case.

